  "question": {
    "text": "Three rods of identical cross-sectional area and made from the same metal, form the sides of an isosceles triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math>&nbsp;right angled at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> as shown in figure. The point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> are maintained at temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>T</mi></math> respectively, in the&nbsp;steady state. Now, assuming that only heat conduction takes place. The temperature of point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi></math> will be<br /><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/bitsat/902d5ed5-7c34-42f9-b93b-f5e60dd206eb-image.png\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 188px;\" />",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "l42xk",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>T</mi><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>",
      "isCorrect": false,
